#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "511342eb-691a-57be-a7cc-f10ab430b575" :question/math-academy-id "e-131" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, differentiable at $x=-1?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/fafd2847f21de3f9f35e05dac61e8a5e406ede40ef7fba014ca5c01eb6d6cdd6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\nThe function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Since it is not continuous at $x=-1,$ it is also not differentiable there."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "68eb6b1f-955c-550b-9232-3c6115ac220f" :question/math-academy-id "e-132" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are differentiable at the point $x=-1?$\n\n- $f(x) = x^5+1$\n\n- $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial and is therefore differentiable and continuous for all $x.$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=-1,$ so it is not differentiable at $x=-1$ either.\n\nIn conclusion, only $f(x)$ is differentiable at the point $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/id #uuid "de600cc4-19fc-53a7-babc-fc387eff718c" :question/math-academy-id "e-133" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted below. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/59499238b529d4d583885cf2c06bae11991c7d3fb5f84f347158e774e2b4ae00.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a vertical tangent, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0, 1, 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26960bd6-f746-5870-bf1d-69df993a2580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280477} {:answer/id #uuid "2594edf5-c303-55e2-af84-7432f5ec8a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280478} {:answer/id #uuid "0f30437e-e83d-53b4-8704-d1ad464b3353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280479} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b8b880-5515-5873-9384-4788d5611d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280480} {:answer/id #uuid "801fe666-f32c-58e0-9865-319e160f96c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5b8b880-5515-5873-9384-4788d5611d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280480} :answer-field/id #uuid "17c89265-c5ce-54dd-a27f-42467b37d23d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7751155c-8e19-5510-944b-ebee6e554dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-101964" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/80fca5dc871cd083e638472abaf26b2a8ba91e81cfa5372df7398b4b63346dfa.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=10$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking a pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=5$ without taking a pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the function is not continuous at $x=10$, it can't be differentiable at that point.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f518edbf-834f-5243-acb7-b41a1a911e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280484} {:answer/id #uuid "c73c7f8f-9f0a-5561-9525-a7954c4cb58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280485} {:answer/id #uuid "5bedc3c0-cb9a-5761-9cb4-aaae6d2f0d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280486} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbc3fe1-8ec0-5c3f-ae5c-702cb7310778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280487} {:answer/id #uuid "78c12121-cb3b-5692-a3e9-e27152d4e6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73c7f8f-9f0a-5561-9525-a7954c4cb58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280485} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebc2692-9d63-50d4-a12f-bee802e69f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71b1d824-b07f-58a9-9408-c51993cdaaec" :question/math-academy-id "q-101983" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/dc27a54ac20efc84bfbc404a20a91309c60c3fb42118f164129e93eee584437b.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=-5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$ it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is continuous at $x=0$ and doesn't have a corner point, a  cusp, or a vertical tangent.\n\nTherefore, only statement III  is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c66f89c-c9df-53cc-8bc8-210bc95e27ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280491} {:answer/id #uuid "b83ce08e-e786-5d04-be3e-2d240cd4c322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280492} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fa0f86-6d26-5c87-bf26-594329bed878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280493} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7406a5-9526-5a34-a50f-21d937be781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280494} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e67f65-9319-5c95-b077-eb490494aa98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4fa0f86-6d26-5c87-bf26-594329bed878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0edc67ed-8886-5543-a970-59a15eaee2d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "002228be-6172-506e-9310-e16f6f583b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-101994" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/ce2ec4b51b4c55975bf25234fdfbfa98663ae95ef7ee2bd5a774c4e4d4655292.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=0$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ff78273-1b14-5ddf-8000-b7b5ce47a464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280498} {:answer/id #uuid "23f8b208-73c1-5119-aee4-9ad695409c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280499} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0988c3-4db4-59ab-bdcc-694e11c5f6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280500} {:answer/id #uuid "d393ac36-bd23-54ba-ae5f-06d6e9ccf259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280501} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2e917c-5b8b-58e8-b018-2127b0763ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ff78273-1b14-5ddf-8000-b7b5ce47a464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280498} :answer-field/id #uuid "94656019-44ee-53c5-8da1-18fbcb22b7e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bec816a-4724-5c20-95e7-b52e7d3f32fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-101995" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ed2876-9c70-5df1-bea3-28a1d84eda17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ x-1,\\, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280505} {:answer/id #uuid "18477b97-c937-5785-977f-8313442363c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280506} {:answer/id #uuid "a054dab7-6cfb-5117-9710-009355c0a7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280507} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb3904f-ab57-574e-95ba-587ffbff42a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280508} {:answer/id #uuid "f2845557-be1e-5715-a89d-8f255fcd1de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt[5]{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcb3904f-ab57-574e-95ba-587ffbff42a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280508} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5d3f4a-4c41-569b-bdf4-aa84204fa7f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46edc91d-e7f5-50a1-b76e-fb0eba0a8481" :question/math-academy-id "q-102159" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $h(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf6e8497-4669-5084-9732-38c2fbe4d251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/q-47669-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114148} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f2438d-6d60-5115-af40-26ada984c0e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/q-47669-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114149} {:answer/id #uuid "36d333d5-7a67-54ef-91f8-131a778f0daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/q-47669-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114150} {:answer/id #uuid "4d064a0e-8c94-5a25-be42-711ce4c45961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/q-47669-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114151} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae424b3-cbc6-57db-ad88-a9fc93750070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/q-47669-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ae424b3-cbc6-57db-ad88-a9fc93750070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/q-47669-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114152} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c90a9f6-9f64-5438-bbf5-7a3d896f8873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2d358ee-4f00-5039-8abe-a47808bd6f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-47669" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ \nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=h(x).$\n\nThe other graphs do not have a vertical tangent inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The functions $y=p(x)$ and $y=q(x)$ are not defined at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e339c004-6f98-5b1a-b6d1-9cb41801eb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512} {:answer/id #uuid "937ff6de-4be2-5339-a241-5d22bb7202d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280513} {:answer/id #uuid "292b4aaf-e572-535c-b1ec-2ff65c941c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} {:answer/id #uuid "c88d96ff-7cc7-5c11-a4c7-ccd3c6a0b983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280515} {:answer/id #uuid "541a4392-08cc-5e82-8c3d-59b4531a9115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541a4392-08cc-5e82-8c3d-59b4531a9115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280516} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e597486-4f3d-5e3d-81fc-c87f5c6f2322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46865db0-9da2-58cc-8805-6fc82c34bf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-47670" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$, is differentiable at $x=2$, and has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is continuous at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=2$ it is also  continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at this point, hence it can't be differentiable at $x=3$ either."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140e4e13-b1ff-5cbb-b34b-6f86109cc2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280519} {:answer/id #uuid "bd58eda1-413f-5637-8d17-0bbb88b3f8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280520} {:answer/id #uuid "3f36fdd6-c55a-5179-8a26-414408ac7d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2|x+2|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280521} {:answer/id #uuid "36ed0c88-6717-5b68-a863-82a514be1196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ x-2,\\, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280522} {:answer/id #uuid "b73f7d30-ca81-5401-b313-fccb31b50d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73f7d30-ca81-5401-b313-fccb31b50d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280523} :answer-field/id #uuid "453aab19-db93-56bf-bd0d-77c5046deb7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d0eb31e-00b8-5cab-baf1-ea4a71c1aca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47680" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all real number $x.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nAll other functions are not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$has a vertical tangent at $x=-2$, and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00ef24b-b0fa-5085-ae3a-db8fea58916d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280526} {:answer/id #uuid "b75398cb-dcef-57d8-a605-625b7e970d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280527} {:answer/id #uuid "c24e39f8-84e1-5a75-bf0e-4a9ffd7fa725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280528} {:answer/id #uuid "536cfb33-9ec5-5e57-9014-9be7d8a50778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280529} {:answer/id #uuid "ae34c201-b60c-5ae3-8ebf-150bc26bd7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae34c201-b60c-5ae3-8ebf-150bc26bd7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280530} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c7b53b-b0d7-5232-a529-b0fbb684c697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0ff0842-96b0-56ec-a561-8c9fa764c1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-47681" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement I is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154a5be4-d06e-5dc2-a3b5-a22669c9b0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/ba2e2109b1cbf4271cc034f55a51382eab026e8316da4618a5479a24eef0ba43.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280533} {:answer/id #uuid "95e8214b-acd1-51ca-8e0c-22e0d6267c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/fb89648926f52a41de06838b4380c70bd3892feb951a8c56bcda0022c887d34f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280534} {:answer/id #uuid "230a67e1-ad40-5230-aa23-f77406c1cf3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/306baf2d3626ebe7f72db4dc87c1f0c0ad5fd2cfa24aaf211e34a25721227cda.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280535} {:answer/id #uuid "ef46f49d-873a-5a10-9a7f-df5d74e2c6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/476f5e8313b5ef82143b00424786c384c3159e1de9586f102655abe13ff1d7d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280536} {:answer/id #uuid "288231d1-cfcf-5c86-9291-2d0d2bd6c7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/c3eee876a16931f73e275222931da77be3d7828753dba53f5db511d0cd0abd94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef46f49d-873a-5a10-9a7f-df5d74e2c6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/476f5e8313b5ef82143b00424786c384c3159e1de9586f102655abe13ff1d7d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280536} :answer-field/id #uuid "06dc3fca-67f9-525f-bdf8-76259e6db818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ccce8a3-bf9d-5a78-aab6-47b5f04c990b" :question/math-academy-id "q-47685" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a cusp?" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=q(x)$ has a cusp at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ So $y=q(x)$ is the correct answer.\n\nThe other graphs do not have a cusp within the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ and is therefore differentiable at every point in the interval.\n\n- The function $y=p(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "623b76d9-dfec-5a8a-8036-e643c5992d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280540} {:answer/id #uuid "b3dfdd94-afce-50db-b653-41884606efb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280541} {:answer/id #uuid "e823b158-fdc8-5fb0-8a68-6bb926fff1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280542} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f0e789-006d-5073-ada4-38800bec46ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280543} {:answer/id #uuid "12791db1-e25e-592c-abcf-2e721d6ec2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e823b158-fdc8-5fb0-8a68-6bb926fff1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280542} :answer-field/id #uuid "d960b9f1-b25b-5805-ba6a-d074e64d57a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a458dca-2a43-5376-8057-5ac8e05b0c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-51070" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/5eae80c03e35f79eaa484a5d54f7fa1a0ec46a5f27480fb69089efab938eba06.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=0$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $f(x)$ has a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3928f90-05d4-52bf-8689-8f21e3acf35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280547} {:answer/id #uuid "f8dc4ddf-3f1d-5539-8806-0470231a9213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} {:answer/id #uuid "622c0c77-e406-5ed5-83a0-9cab85998855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280549} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c90a39-2bbb-5c26-860f-1ac7254e4977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280550} {:answer/id #uuid "c715d098-2dec-58ae-a246-5b44bb4d4c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c715d098-2dec-58ae-a246-5b44bb4d4c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280551} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ea2c2c-58bb-5361-ae54-875c556c7311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eccc4e3-a9e3-5459-9bf6-9ea806485f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-73250" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/a8a70a2f28585b63d2a083b42eb6ad54bfb581b5f13c699a3bc3c8d836afaee8.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67540de9-55ee-5afe-8643-f637b91364b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280554} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e913d7-323b-54cd-aaed-48a78b335904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280555} {:answer/id #uuid "4047646c-a17f-5165-9c91-a3958a3c0cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280556} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab6056c-76ee-53f7-b25f-ae08303549a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280557} {:answer/id #uuid "56f81cd6-629c-57e8-ad3f-5bcb408fce0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67540de9-55ee-5afe-8643-f637b91364b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280554} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a86a6f7-327b-5b40-9833-09d09f653150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0101e9c4-90bb-5c1d-b6a4-c53b0c99dd90" :question/math-academy-id "q-73252" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/1f2d8b9faac6c300b41902cea5065bf8bafb02c137232ee79779a4dc3ff67f33.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ So, it can't be differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III  are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19881840-23cb-5bd9-af51-c86a13510dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280561} {:answer/id #uuid "52d638f2-0a10-50f1-9c4a-defa261ce77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280562} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1d8d21-9a86-5967-ac82-24017082b5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280563} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf8a5e6-9613-5a14-844b-a46eb9ce6338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280564} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd0f9c3-2d18-57a4-92b9-691a28014f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b1d8d21-9a86-5967-ac82-24017082b5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280563} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1287e4-30db-5043-afeb-beaf61b74880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68d793ec-aaf3-5b37-877d-af06a0a967e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73267" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9408aea0-188c-576f-9cbc-30b4f7d7dcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280568} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbfa959-c4b3-57a8-8635-ab5704290f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280569} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4a061c-1dce-539c-bb12-aaa3ed87a30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280570} {:answer/id #uuid "b971f998-7ede-5e2a-997e-b66429b4ce86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280571} {:answer/id #uuid "328d4ec9-fc9a-58ef-a77f-873385df118f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b971f998-7ede-5e2a-997e-b66429b4ce86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280571} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d977f7-1583-58e8-898d-6a3d5a2b8050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d3d55e7-45c3-56ac-8489-9d0883fda9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73270" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I and II are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}]}
